From 24a52ac6a87f34ac6e30e494622afc2b323584b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 19:28:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Give a module's top-level lexicals their own environment
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A module's top-level let/const/class bindings were published into the realm's
shared global lexical environment, the same as a script's. That made every
module share one realm-wide slot per name, so a module that declared a
top-level `const x` and, while its body was still running, triggered a
transitive import of another module that also declared a top-level `const x`
hit the first module's read-only binding and threw "Cannot assign to read only
variable". Sibling imports at one level escaped only because each body had
returned before the next ran.

Keep a module's top-level lexicals local to its compiled body: gate the
global-lexical publishing in VisitProgram on the ES module goal (the ambient
the parser already reads), and collect the names an `export const/let/class`
introduces so an exported declaration follows the same module-local path as a
bare one rather than falling through to a global-lexical slot. This is also the
spec-correct scoping — an indirect eval in the global environment must not see
a module's top-level bindings.

Separately, make `export *` respect export precedence: a star re-export no
longer overwrites a name the module already exports locally or via a named
re-export (ResolveExport consults local/indirect entries before star entries),
which also removes the read-only-property throw when a star followed a local
export.

Regressions in ModuleScopeIsolationTests.
